But wait, I'm seeing the real problem now: `class_id` contains dots itself (like `AF-SCC-C2-VAC-GEN`), so splitting on `.` and taking only the first part gives just `AF`, which doesn't exist in the dictionary. I need to split on the first dot to separate the prefix from the rest, not the last dot. Let me fix that with `split(".", 1)` instead.

For C2, I'm also reconsidering my logic — the token `verified_by_L1` might legitimately survive if the ledger has been updated to include rows with that verification status, so I should check the ledger first before flagging the token as over-strong.

For C6, I'm noticing three differences in the data: F1 has an extra `(weighted Sobolev)` parenthetical in the `spaces` field, F2a is missing the word `and` before `delta in (1/2,1)`, and more critically F1 includes `delta in (1/2,1)` in its `data_regularity` while F2a/F2b don't specify the delta range at all.

The core question is whether these are cosmetic or substantive — dropping the delta constraint from `data_regularity` is a real narrowing since delta is the key weighted-Sobolev exponent, so if F1 says delta ∈ (1/2,1) and F2a/F2b don't, they're not sharing a single frozen data class.

I should make the checker more rigorous: compare the normative fields with a principled normalization that handles prose differences (case, whitespace, punctuation) but flags any delta-range omission as a substantive difference. Let me report both the whitespace-normalized comparison and a strictly-tokenized comparison, then fix the three bugs and re-run.

